proof.text:
Write $b_u^{\Et}(W)=W	imes_VU$, $b_u^{\Proet}(W)=W	imes_VU$, and $i_T:T_{\Et}	o T_{\Proet}$ for the inclusion. The two functors $b_u^{\Proet}i_V$ and $i_Ub_u^{\Et}$ have canonically identical objects and arrows: their fibre products have the same projections. The etale base-change continuity statement and the pro-etale inverse-image definition ensure that their restrictions carry sheaves to sheaves. By the continuous-functor sheaf adjunction, $
u_{T,*}$ is restriction along $i_T$. Therefore, for a pro-etale sheaf $G$ on $U$ and an etale object $W/V$, both $(
u_{V,*}u_{\Proet,*}G)(W)$ and $(u_{\Et,*}
u_{U,*}G)(W)$ are $G(W	imes_VU)$, and on a morphism $a:W_1	o W_2$ both restrictions are $G(a	imes_V\mathrm{id}_U)$. This constructs a natural isomorphism $\delta_u:
u_{V,*}u_{\Proet,*}\xrightarrow{\sim}u_{\Et,*}
u_{U,*}$. For abelian sheaves the general sheaf adjunction identifies comparison direct image as the same restriction. For module sheaves take the finite discrete ring $\Lambda$ of [def-weil-pbc-site-comparison-morphism]. By [def-weil-pbc-direct-image] and [def-weil-proetale-inverse-image], the direct-image coefficient action is induced by sending $a\in\Lambda$ to its constant section on the base-changed object. Both composites send $a$ to that same section on $W	imes_VU$, so $\delta_u$ and its inverse are $\Lambda$-linear.

Let $L_1=
u_U^*u_{\Et}^{-1}$, $R_1=u_{\Et,*}
u_{U,*}$ and $L_2=u_{\Proet}^{-1}
u_V^*$, $R_2=
u_{V,*}u_{\Proet,*}$. The inverse-image and comparison adjunctions, composed by [def-weil-pbc-composite-adjunction], give $L_1\dashv R_1$ and $L_2\dashv R_2$. For sheaves of sets use the etale base-change continuity and [lem-weil-pbc-continuous-functor-sheaf-pullback-adjunction] to obtain the etale inverse-image adjunction; for abelian and fixed module sheaves use [def-weil-pbc-inverse-image]. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-conjugate-iso-unit-equation] to these adjunctions and $\delta_u$ from step 1. It gives the natural isomorphism $c_u:L_1\xrightarrow{\sim}L_2$, uniquely normalized by $R_1(c_{u,F})\eta_{1,F}=\delta_{u,L_2F}\eta_{2,F}$. Equivalently, for every $F,G$ the induced bijection $\operatorname{Hom}(L_2F,G)	o\operatorname{Hom}(L_1F,G)$ is the chain $\operatorname{Hom}(L_2F,G)\cong\operatorname{Hom}(F,R_2G)\xrightarrow{\delta_u}\operatorname{Hom}(F,R_1G)\cong\operatorname{Hom}(L_1F,G)$. This proves the displayed isomorphism, with its specified normalization rather than an arbitrary choice of isomorphism.
